{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2190-09-26 02:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi, I'm Dr. Lee. I’ll be helping take care of you tonight. Can you tell me what brought you to the hospital?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2190-09-26 02:31:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Hi, Doctor. My blood sugar was really high yesterday—over 400. I gave myself extra insulin, but this morning I started feeling nauseous and had some belly pain."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2190-09-26 02:32:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Okay. Did you notice anything else? Any vomiting, fever, or trouble breathing?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2190-09-26 02:33:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "No vomiting or diarrhea. Just the nausea and pain. I did check my urine at home and saw a little ketones."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2190-09-26 02:34:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I see. You’re on an insulin pump for type 1 diabetes, correct? And you’ve had two kidney transplants?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2190-09-26 02:35:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, that’s right. I’ve had no DKA before, so I was worried when I saw the ketones."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2190-09-26 02:36:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s a good instinct. We’ve checked your labs, and your blood sugar was 638 when you arrived, with signs of mild diabetic ketoacidosis. We’ve started you on IV fluids and an insulin drip to correct that."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2190-09-26 02:37:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Oh wow, that high? I didn’t feel that bad yesterday. Is the DKA dangerous?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2190-09-26 02:38:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It can be if not treated. But we caught it early. The fluids and insulin are helping. Your acid levels are improving. We’re keeping you in the ICU for close monitoring tonight."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2190-09-26 02:39:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, that makes sense. I’ve been on cefpodoxime for a UTI a week ago. Could that be related?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2190-09-26 02:40:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Possibly. Infections can trigger DKA, even if you don’t feel very sick. Your urine culture last week showed E. coli, but it was sensitive to the antibiotic you’re on. We’re continuing it since you’re immunocompromised from your transplant."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2190-09-26 02:41:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I usually don’t have symptoms with UTIs. But I did notice some swelling in my legs lately."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2190-09-26 02:42:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We saw that. Your kidney function was a bit off—creatinine was 1.2—but it’s improved with fluids. An ultrasound showed your transplant kidney looks good, with normal blood flow."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2190-09-26 02:43:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s a relief. I’ve had urosepsis before, so I get nervous."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2190-09-26 02:44:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Understandable. But your vitals are stable, and you’re not showing signs of sepsis now. We’re giving IV ceftriaxone in addition to your oral antibiotic just to be safe."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2190-09-26 04:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I’m back to check on you. How are you feeling now?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2190-09-26 04:16:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Better. The belly pain is gone, and I’m not nauseous anymore."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2190-09-26 04:17:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Great. Your labs are improving. Glucose is down to 346, and your acid levels are getting closer to normal. We’ve lowered the insulin drip and will keep adjusting it."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2190-09-26 04:18:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Will I go back on my pump soon?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2190-09-26 04:19:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, once your sugars stabilize. The diabetes team will help adjust your pump settings to prevent this from happening again."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2190-09-26 06:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. I wanted to talk about something we noticed. Your troponin levels are slightly elevated and went up over night."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2190-09-26 06:31:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Troponin? That’s for heart attacks, right? I didn’t have any chest pain."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2190-09-26 06:32:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. You didn’t have symptoms, and your EKG is normal. This is likely a ‘type 2’ heart attack—meaning the heart had extra stress from the DKA and dehydration, not a blocked artery. The levels peaked and are now coming down."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2190-09-26 06:33:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So it’s not a full heart attack? But I still need to follow up?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2190-09-26 06:34:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Correct. It’s a stress-related enzyme rise. But given your diabetes and history, you should see a cardiologist as an outpatient to be safe."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2190-09-26 10:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your insulin drip is almost off. We’re transitioning you back to your pump. The diabetes team adjusted your basal rates slightly to help prevent high sugars."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2190-09-26 10:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "What changed?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2190-09-26 10:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "They increased the morning basal rate a bit—from 0.825 to 0.88 units per hour—and kept the rest similar. Your correction factor is still 1 unit per 50 mg/dL."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2190-09-26 10:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, I can update that. I’ve been trying to avoid lows, so maybe I under-corrected snacks."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2190-09-26 10:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s actually a common cause. With the UTI, your body needed more insulin, but if you didn’t adjust, it could lead to DKA. The team suggests discussing adding long-acting insulin at home to prevent this."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2190-09-26 14:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re doing well. We’re moving you to the regular floor. Your blood sugar is stable on the pump, and your kidney function has improved."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2190-09-26 14:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s great. Do I keep taking the antibiotic?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2190-09-26 14:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, finish the full two-week course of cefpodoxime. Even though you feel fine, it’s important for your transplant health."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2190-09-27 08:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good news—you’re ready to go home today. Your labs are much better, and you’ve been stable overnight."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2190-09-27 08:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s wonderful. What do I need to do next?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2190-09-27 08:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "First, call your endocrinologist within the next two weeks to review the pump changes and talk about adding long-acting insulin. Also, schedule a cardiology follow-up for the troponin issue."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2190-09-27 08:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And my thyroid medicine? I’ve been taking 112 mcg daily, but I think my dose was supposed to change."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2190-09-27 08:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, continue with 112 mcg for now and discuss the change with your endocrinologist. All your other meds—like cyclosporine, mycophenolate, and aspirin—stay the same."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2190-09-27 08:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Got it. Thank you for explaining everything."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2190-09-27 08:06:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. Take care, and we’ll see you in follow-up if needed."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2190-09-26 02:28:00",
    "admission_end": "2190-09-27 17:20:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "A woman with type 1 diabetes, a history of two kidney transplants, and a recent UTI was admitted for hyperglycemia and mild diabetic ketoacidosis. She presented with blood glucose in the 400s, abdominal pain, nausea, and trace ketones. She was treated with IV fluids and an insulin drip, which resolved the DKA. Her insulin pump was restarted with adjusted basal rates to prevent recurrence. During admission, she had a troponin rise peaking at 0.94 ng/mL without symptoms or EKG changes, consistent with a type 2 NSTEMI likely due to metabolic stress from DKA and hypovolemia. She continued treatment for a complicated UTI with cefpodoxime, and her renal transplant function improved with hydration. She was discharged home in stable condition with plans to follow up with endocrinology and cardiology.",
    "problems": [
      "Type 1 diabetes mellitus with ketoacidosis without coma",
      "Non-ST elevation (NSTEMI) myocardial infarction",
      "Cystitis, unspecified without hematuria",
      "Kidney transplant status",
      "Hypovolemia"
    ]
  }
}
